    <string name="theory_progress">Completed %1$d / %2$d</string>
    <string name="theory_notes_title">Notes and semitones</string>
    <string name="theory_notes_body">Notes are named C D E F G A B; with sharps/flats there are 12 per octave. The smallest step is a semitone.</string>
    <string name="theory_major_title">Major scale</string>
    <string name="theory_major_body">A major scale follows W-W-H-W-W-W-H. On C it uses only the white keys.</string>
    <string name="theory_minor_title">Minor scale</string>
    <string name="theory_minor_body">The natural minor scale is W-H-W-W-H-W-W, giving a softer, darker sound. A minor uses only white keys.</string>
    <string name="theory_interval_title">Intervals</string>
    <string name="theory_interval_body">An interval is the distance between two notes. Thirds and fifths are most common; a perfect fifth sounds open and stable.</string>
    <string name="theory_triad_title">Triads</string>
    <string name="theory_triad_body">A triad stacks a root, third and fifth. Major sounds bright, minor soft and diminished tense.</string>
    <string name="theory_key_title">Keys and Camelot</string>
    <string name="theory_key_body">The tonic defines the key. DJs use the Camelot wheel; neighbouring numbers mix harmonically (built in here).</string>
    <string name="theory_progression_title">Common progressions</string>
    <string name="theory_progression_body">I–V–vi–IV is one of the most common pop progressions, for example C–G–Am–F.</string>
    <string name="theory_penta_title">Pentatonic scale</string>
    <string name="theory_penta_body">The pentatonic scale drops the 4th and 7th degrees, leaving five notes often used in Chinese-style melodies.</string>
